#{[:db/ident :activity.type/assignment {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461 :db/ident :activity.type/assignment}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/free {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167 :db/ident :answer-field.type/free}] [:db/ident :answer.type/math {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math}] [:db/ident :answer.type/text {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}] [:db/ident :course-academy.source/editor {:db/doc "User-directed content formatting edits in Course Academy." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502323 :db/ident :course-academy.source/editor}] [:course/id #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235" {:course/code "MTH 256" :course/description "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms." :course/id #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b27b909d-406b-5016-9dba-6d6cde8fe738" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Apply introductory solution methods to higher-order linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406528} {:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "f3d63c4b-0f30-5288-8faf-fd7effcd7d55" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Obtain solutions of second-order linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406529} {:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "5879a21c-ee5c-5351-9547-766af3a0a0b4" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Solve first-order linear equations and selected first-order nonlinear equations using methods appropriate to their form." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406530} {:course-outcome/category "Electrical and Mechanical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "7677d590-eded-5f0e-a320-fd5a22fef284" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve differential-equation problems involving electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators, interpreting the resulting functions in terms of the modeled system." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406531} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a8623707-6d3b-55ce-b413-a7dcb5686dd4" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Interpret the variables, derivatives, and solutions of a differential-equation model in the context of the problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406532} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b3f87601-f5f0-5265-a60a-d5faa7f8ef26" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Classify ordinary differential equations by order and distinguish linear equations from nonlinear equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406533} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "06f1cd43-7150-5138-9257-b06e58024ccf" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Translate a basic scientific or engineering model into a differential equation relating an unknown function to its rates of change." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406534} {:course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d14ff5cc-c727-5ac4-80dc-396644cb4d2d" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Explain how a Laplace transform can convert a linear differential-equation problem into an algebraic problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406535} {:course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "1a7956a5-b684-551c-83df-ccea47ac1ba4" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Use elementary Laplace transforms and inverse transforms to solve basic linear differential-equation problems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406536}] :course/overview "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Applied Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205}] [:topic/math-academy-id 285 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777 :topic/difficulty 0.6889849861565911 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428780 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not i